WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:10.080
The scene we are about to see was written by Homer&nbsp;
almost three thousand years ago in the Odyssey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:13.120
one of the most important poems in all of history.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:17.520
And today it is remembered as one&nbsp;
of the saddest stories ever told.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:23.840
The story tells that after twenty years away from&nbsp;
his home, Ulysses finally returned. But he did so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:30.560
disguised as an old beggar, so that no one would&nbsp;
recognize him. Not even his wife, nor his own son.

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:36.800
Except one.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:40.000
His dog Argos. The same puppy Ulysses had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:44.560
adopted and raised twenty long years&nbsp;
earlier, before leaving for the war.

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:49.680
When the two were reunited, Argos was&nbsp;
already a very old dog. He was thin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:55.200
sick, and without the strength to stand up.&nbsp;
But his eyes did not lie. He had recognized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:59.920
his owner immediately, and looked at&nbsp;
him with the same devotion as always.

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:05.520
Ulysses gazed at him for a moment with a broken&nbsp;
heart, but he could not bend down to pet him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:10.480
nor call him by his name. Any gesture&nbsp;
of affection toward Argos would reveal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:15.600
his identity, and that could cost him&nbsp;
his life and the future of his land

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:18.800
This work is called Ulysses and Argos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:23.840
and it was painted by the British&nbsp;
artist Briton Rivière in 1885.

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:35.440
Ulysses was the king of Ithaca. The story&nbsp;
tells that he had a dog named Argos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:40.800
whom he raised from a puppy and trained as&nbsp;
a hunting dog. Argos was young, fast, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:46.800
strong. But before he could go hunting with his&nbsp;
master, Ulysses had to leave for the Trojan War.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:51.120
The war lasted ten years. And&nbsp;
the journey home, another ten.

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:56.000
During those two decades, everyone&nbsp;
in Ithaca gave Ulysses up for dead.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:00.560
His palace was invaded by suitors&nbsp;
seeking to marry his wife Penelope.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:04.640
His servants stopped waiting for him,&nbsp;
and Argos was completely abandoned.

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:10.400
When Homer describes him, twenty years later,&nbsp;
Argos lies on a pile of manure in front of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:16.800
palace. Old, weak, infested with parasites,&nbsp;
and without the strength to even stand up.

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:20.160
Until one day, a beggar approaches the palace.

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:26.240
That beggar is Ulysses. The master he had&nbsp;
waited so many years for has finally returned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:31.360
but disguised as a beggar, since he needs&nbsp;
to hide his identity to reclaim his throne&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:36.880
from the suitors who invaded his home. No&nbsp;
one can know who he is. Not his servants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:39.760
not his enemies, and not even his own wife.

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:43.120
No one recognizes him. Except Argos.

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:47.360
The instant the old dog sees the&nbsp;
beggar approaching, he recognizes him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:53.200
immediately. After twenty years, and despite&nbsp;
the disguise, the old age, and all the time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.200 --> 00:03:00.000
that has passed. Argos tries to get up to go to&nbsp;
his master. But he does not have the strength.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:03.520
Now that we know the story,&nbsp;
let us return to the painting.

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:08.480
Of all the possible moments in this scene,&nbsp;
Briton Rivière chose the most devastating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:14.160
one. He painted the exact instant in which&nbsp;
the master and the dog look at each other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:17.520
recognize each other, and can do nothing about it.

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:21.920
In the painting, Ulysses stands wrapped in the&nbsp;
rags of his disguise, leaning on his staff. His&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:26.000
face is turned toward the dog, but&nbsp;
his body remains oriented forward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:30.000
surely toward the palace to&nbsp;
continue his mission. In the work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:36.400
he is literally caught between two directions.&nbsp;
Between what he feels and what he must do.

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:40.560
When we look at the ground, we see&nbsp;
Argos lifting his head toward him.

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:46.720
Rivière paints him as an old dog, exactly as&nbsp;
he is described in Homer's Odyssey. His bones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:52.960
show beneath his skin. His body can barely move.&nbsp;
But his gaze is completely fixed on his master,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:57.120
and his posture, with his ears dropped&nbsp;
and his neck stretched toward him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:02.240
conveys that final effort to reach the&nbsp;
person he waited for his entire life.

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:08.960
Briton Rivière was probably the only artist of his&nbsp;
time capable of painting this scene in this way.

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:14.720
Born in London in 1840, he dedicated&nbsp;
practically his entire career to painting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:19.200
animals. As we have seen in other&nbsp;
paintings analyzed on this channel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:22.800
animals were the emotional&nbsp;
protagonists of his works.

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:28.080
His method was obsessive. He himself explained&nbsp;
that the only way to paint animals was to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:33.600
gradually accumulate a large number of studies&nbsp;
and a deep knowledge of the animal itself.

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:38.960
He spent countless hours at the London&nbsp;
zoological gardens, studying animal anatomy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:43.840
observing how they move, how they look,&nbsp;
and how they express what they feel.

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:48.400
What Rivière achieved with this dog is&nbsp;
something very few artists have accomplished,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:52.960
and that is painting genuine emotion&nbsp;
in an animal without humanizing it.

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:59.040
Three years after this work, Rivière would paint&nbsp;
Requiescat, another of his most famous paintings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:03.520
in which a dog keeps watch over&nbsp;
the body of his dead master. Thus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:09.200
turning the loyalty of dogs in the face of&nbsp;
absence into the great theme of his career.

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:11.760
But there is something in the story of Argos that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:15.360
has kept this scene alive for&nbsp;
almost three thousand years.

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:20.240
Of Homer's entire poem, with its&nbsp;
gods, its monsters, and its battles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:24.880
this small episode of just a few lines&nbsp;
is one of the most remembered. The poet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:28.880
Alexander Pope translated it into&nbsp;
English in the eighteenth century&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:34.160
and turned it into one of the most quoted&nbsp;
passages in all of literature about dogs.

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:40.000
And the reason is simple. Argos represents&nbsp;
something we all recognize: loyalty that asks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:44.960
for nothing in return. A dog that did not wait&nbsp;
twenty years because he had hope, nor because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:50.960
he understood where his master was, but because&nbsp;
for Argos, there was no other possible option.

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:55.680
Scholars of the Odyssey point out that&nbsp;
Argos is also a mirror of Ulysses himself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.680 --> 00:06:00.560
Both were young and strong, and in turn,&nbsp;
both were also forgotten by their world.

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:06.960
The difference is that Ulysses was able to recover&nbsp;
his life, his throne, and his family. Argos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:13.120
on the other hand, only hoped to see him one&nbsp;
last time. And the moment he did, he died.

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:18.080
Something else worth mentioning about this&nbsp;
work is what is happening with it right now.

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:22.240
Just a few weeks ago, Christopher&nbsp;
Nolan's The Odyssey premiered,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:27.040
one of the most anticipated films of&nbsp;
recent years. And with it, millions of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:31.840
people around the world are discovering&nbsp;
the story of Ulysses for the first time.

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:37.280
Because of this, this painting by Rivière has&nbsp;
begun to circulate massively on the internet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:42.720
being presented as one of the saddest paintings&nbsp;
in the history of art. Thanks to this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:47.760
many people are discovering it for the first&nbsp;
time, which is wonderful. But the reality is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:52.240
that this image has been moving those who&nbsp;
contemplate it for more than a century.

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:58.800
When Rivière painted it in 1885, he was already&nbsp;
one of the most recognized artists in England. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:04.000
was a member of the Royal Academy, the most&nbsp;
prestigious art institution in the country,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:09.840
and his works were exhibited before thousands of&nbsp;
people. In fact, his mastery of animal painting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:15.920
was so respected that the University of Oxford&nbsp;
awarded him an honorary doctorate years later.

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:20.240
And this makes me think about something.&nbsp;
How many works around the world never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:24.800
receive the recognition they deserve&nbsp;
simply because of the passage of time?

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:28.640
The fact that a painting inspired by&nbsp;
one of the most important poems in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:33.520
history was almost completely forgotten&nbsp;
tells us a lot about the fate of so many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:39.040
other wonderful works. And sadly, many&nbsp;
of them will remain forgotten forever.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:42.800
Because not all of them will have&nbsp;
a movie to bring them back to life.

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:46.560
